1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:12,640
47 square miles of technical virtuosity, including 14 miles of the world's most heavily traveled monorail.

2
00:00:12,641 --> 00:00:17,600
A tree 14 stories tall that's wrapped around an oil rig.

3
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:24,560
Linear synchronous motors that launch a vehicle from 0 to 60 miles per hour in 3 seconds.

4
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:27,160
9 acres of hidden tunnels.

5
00:00:27,161 --> 00:00:35,500
Journey underground, backstage and backstory at the technological marvel that is Walt Disney World.

6
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:37,900
Next on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:37,901 --> 00:00:51,901
A vista into a world of wondrous ideas

8
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:58,080
Signifying man's achievement

9
00:00:58,081 --> 00:00:59,820
A step into the future

10
00:00:59,821 --> 00:01:02,720
With predictions of constructive things to come

11
00:01:02,721 --> 00:01:14,120
Enter a make-believe world spanning some 27,000 acres and brought to life by cutting-edge technology.

12
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:21,680
What was once Swampland now boasts the world's largest theme park.

13
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:32,580
The ride technology ranges from space-age centrifuges to enhanced motion vehicles powered by 3,000 PSI of hydraulic pressure.

14
00:01:32,720 --> 00:01:40,720
And hundreds of audio animatronics live through the power of pneumatics, hydraulics and electrical systems.

15
00:01:40,721 --> 00:01:50,720
Our technology in the field of robotics really is evolutionary beginning 40 years ago and continues today building on that.

16
00:01:50,721 --> 00:01:56,720
Walt Disney World is made up of four separate theme parks, each with its own innovations.

17
00:01:56,721 --> 00:02:06,540
The first, the 107-acre Magic Kingdom, was born in secrecy when Disney purchased thousands of acres of land anonymously.

18
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:12,620
The Magic Kingdom conceals complex tunnels that are the invisible arteries of a park.

19
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:21,880
Transportation, costuming, computer controls, and even a high-speed garbage collection system are all hidden 14 feet below ground.

20
00:02:21,881 --> 00:02:29,240
Epcot, which is three times the size of the Magic Kingdom, is a never-ending World's Fair,

21
00:02:29,241 --> 00:02:32,640
a tribute to science, technology and world culture.

22
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:38,920
At the entrance soars the 18-story tall Spaceship Earth,

23
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:44,440
a 16-million-pound geodesic sphere glittering with 11,000 facets.

24
00:02:44,441 --> 00:02:53,000
A new attraction called Soarin' gives visitors the sights, smells and best of all, the feel of hang gliding over California.

25
00:02:54,960 --> 00:03:04,060
And at the end of every day, the Illumination Show unleashes propane flames and over 1,000 computer-time fireworks

26
00:03:04,061 --> 00:03:10,220
and sends a 350,000-pound globe spinning across the park's central lagoon.

27
00:03:10,221 --> 00:03:14,860
That particular sequence of pyrotechnic effects had never been attempted before.

28
00:03:14,861 --> 00:03:22,900
Just a few miles away, Disney MGM Studios is a salute to the golden days of Hollywood.

29
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:30,420
The Twilight Zone Tower of Terror is a haunted hotel with one extremely unpredictable elevator.

30
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:38,340
Thanks to an advanced T4 computer program, each lurching journey is completely different.

31
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:47,260
The computer will actually decide for you and associated with that is not only is the

32
00:03:47,261 --> 00:03:51,540
motion changed, but all the shell elements are changed.

33
00:03:51,541 --> 00:03:56,880
The Lights Motors Action Extreme Stunt Show sends lightweight, high-speed cars spinning

34
00:03:56,881 --> 00:04:07,240
540 degrees and sailing off ramps both forward and backward.

35
00:04:07,241 --> 00:04:13,180
The newest park, the 500-acre Disney's Animal Kingdom, is almost five times the size of

36
00:04:13,181 --> 00:04:15,460
the Magic Kingdom.

37
00:04:15,461 --> 00:04:20,440
Disney planted four million plants to turn a Central Florida cow pasture into an African

38
00:04:20,441 --> 00:04:21,440
savanna.

39
00:04:21,441 --> 00:04:30,280
And the park's central icon has a very unusual structural support, an oil rig.

40
00:04:30,281 --> 00:04:36,240
On the 100-acre safari ride, African animals look like they're wandering free.

41
00:04:36,241 --> 00:04:38,360
In fact, it is a show set.

42
00:04:38,500 --> 00:04:43,760
It's a series of scenes that have been constructed through the manipulation of the landscape environment.

43
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:48,040
Disney's Animal Kingdom is also home to Expedition Everest.

44
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:55,320
At nearly 200 feet tall, it towers over Disney's other mountains, Thunder, Space, and Splash,

45
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:58,600
and is home to a high-tech, high-speed roller coaster.

46
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:03,220
The four parks are all part of this megaplex of a resort.

47
00:05:03,221 --> 00:05:14,404
Twice the size of Manhattan, it was the final vision and crowning achievement of a man who spent more than 40 years pushing the limits of technology to create

48
00:05:14,405 --> 00:05:15,820
entertainment magic.

49
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:17,940
Walt Disney

50
00:05:17,941 --> 00:05:25,040
Born in 1901, Walter Elias Disney spent his youth in Missouri.

51
00:05:26,040 --> 00:05:29,340
From an early age, Walt wanted to be a cartoonist.

52
00:05:29,620 --> 00:05:32,860
And by his 20s, he had his own Hollywood animation studio.

53
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:40,220
His partner was his brother Roy, who ran the business side of the operation.

54
00:05:40,221 --> 00:05:46,220
From the very beginning, Walt used cutting-edge technology to create more compelling stories.

55
00:05:46,221 --> 00:05:53,220
In 1928, he produced Steamboat Willie, the world's first animated talkie.

56
00:05:53,221 --> 00:06:01,040
In 1932, he dazzled audiences with Flowers and Trees,

57
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:03,440
the world's first cartoon in Technicolor,

58
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:05,520
and the first to win an Oscar.

59
00:06:07,580 --> 00:06:09,140
And with Snow White,

60
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:13,580
he proved that animation was strong enough to carry a feature-length film.

61
00:06:13,940 --> 00:06:15,580
I said, how do you do?

62
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:17,520
How do you do what?

63
00:06:17,521 --> 00:06:23,440
He was about creating ever increasingly immersive stories.

64
00:06:24,120 --> 00:06:26,700
So he took storybooks and created animated films.

65
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:33,520
And in each of those animated films, he continued to push innovative ways to increase the immersion into those films.

66
00:06:33,660 --> 00:06:36,760
So stereo sound, color, feature length.

67
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:38,620
He always hired the best.

68
00:06:38,621 --> 00:06:44,560
And Blood was always pushing them to do more and more and plus it, as they said.

69
00:06:44,561 --> 00:06:50,160
you know, plus it in the way that the scenes are done and the way the animation moves.

70
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:54,060
And they did better work than they thought they were capable of.

71
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:58,420
He may have been an artist, but Disney thought like an engineer.

72
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:02,860
When he wanted to create a greater depth of field in his animated films,

73
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:07,720
and thus a greater sense of realism, he worked with his staff to come up with a solution.

74
00:07:08,720 --> 00:07:14,000
In just a few months, they invented and patented a sophisticated animation camera

75
00:07:14,001 --> 00:07:16,900
called the multi-plane camera.

76
00:07:16,901 --> 00:07:22,240
The individual pieces of artwork are stacked certain distances apart depending on how deep

77
00:07:22,241 --> 00:07:26,400
you want the scene to look, and the camera is placed above, and the pieces of artwork

78
00:07:26,401 --> 00:07:32,000
are moved in towards the camera to simulate the camera panning through dimensional space.

79
00:07:32,001 --> 00:07:38,580
Walt's next leap in storytelling took him out of the animator studio and into a California

80
00:07:38,581 --> 00:07:41,120
orange grove.

81
00:07:41,121 --> 00:07:46,760
In the early 1940s, as he sat on a dirty park bench and watched his daughters ride a carousel,

82
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:48,740
Walt had an idea.

83
00:07:49,780 --> 00:07:53,760
There should be something built, some kind of an amusement enterprise built

84
00:07:53,761 --> 00:07:57,680
where the parents and the children could have fun together.

85
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:02,500
He decided to create a place unlike anything the world had ever seen.

86
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:08,140
In 1953, he purchased 160 acres near Anaheim, California

87
00:08:08,141 --> 00:08:11,200
and set about building the world's first theme park.

88
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:16,440
He gathered up some of his most creative talent and dubbed them Imagineers.

89
00:08:17,140 --> 00:08:20,800
On our first television program, we showed you a blueprint for a dream.

90
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:24,720
Well, this is the blueprint, and the dream is Disneyland.

91
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:31,920
Walt decided early on that the park's various attractions should radiate outward from a convenient central hub.

92
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:35,560
Guests would be drawn to the hub by a dramatic landmark.

93
00:08:35,561 --> 00:08:43,780
And given the success of Disney's first feature-length animated film, Snow White, the landmark had to be a castle.

94
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:48,960
This soaring castle would be surrounded by rides themed to Disney movies.

95
00:08:50,260 --> 00:08:55,000
Park construction began July 21, 1954 at a feverish pace.

96
00:08:55,760 --> 00:09:00,240
The early days of the construction of Disneyland seemed sort of like a heartless task.

97
00:09:00,241 --> 00:09:09,200
It was nothing but a bunch of orange trees coming down and dirt and bulldozers and chaos and construction equipment and cement trucks.

98
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:12,100
I've never seen so much madness in my life.

99
00:09:13,820 --> 00:09:19,280
The park opened less than one year later with all the fanfare Walt Disney could muster.

100
00:09:20,540 --> 00:09:23,520
But it was far from his greatest premiere.

101
00:09:27,040 --> 00:09:28,580
It was a disaster.

102
00:09:28,581 --> 00:09:34,060
In the first place, Walt could control everything except the weather.

103
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:38,420
And it was one of the hottest days of the year in Anaheim.

104
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:45,160
So the women's spiked heels were sinking into the freshly laid asphalt.

105
00:09:45,940 --> 00:09:47,840
They ran out of soft drinks.

106
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:50,760
There were not enough drinking fountains.

107
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:53,080
The rides broke down.

108
00:09:54,080 --> 00:09:58,000
Some smart fella counterfeited about 10,000 tickets.

109
00:09:58,580 --> 00:10:02,880
And so the place was jammed, and it was a mess.

110
00:10:04,560 --> 00:10:08,720
And the next day, it was like, oh, my goodness, we have to go down there again.

111
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:11,040
Everything's broken. We're going to have to take it apart.

112
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:14,240
We're going to have to fix it. And we were in for a long summer.

113
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:17,240
The summer wasn't that long.

114
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:23,260
After a few weeks of ironing out the kinks, Walt's new park became a smash hit.

115
00:10:23,980 --> 00:10:28,340
Every day, thousands of people came to see mechanical animals lurking in the jungle.

116
00:10:28,580 --> 00:10:35,580
Dumbo's that really flew, and a 5-8th scale Main Street that felt like the real thing.

117
00:10:35,581 --> 00:10:41,580
Once again, Walt had succeeded in combining technology with great storytelling.

118
00:10:41,581 --> 00:10:48,580
The theme park is the first experience wherein the audience can actually walk through the frame,

119
00:10:48,581 --> 00:10:53,580
into the story, and participate in that story in a way that was entirely unique.

120
00:10:53,581 --> 00:10:57,580
It went from being a passive experience to an active experience.

121
00:10:58,580 --> 00:11:01,360
Disneyland was an unqualified success.

122
00:11:02,220 --> 00:11:04,260
But Walt still wasn't satisfied.

123
00:11:05,340 --> 00:11:08,800
Outside the gates of his carefully constructed make-believe world,

124
00:11:08,801 --> 00:11:13,840
a disorganized mass of hotels, motor courts, and diners sprang up,

125
00:11:14,180 --> 00:11:15,460
breaking the Disney magic.

126
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:19,940
And inside, practical concerns like garbage collection

127
00:11:19,941 --> 00:11:23,140
and employee transportation also spoiled the illusion.

128
00:11:24,600 --> 00:11:26,420
Walt knew he could do better.

129
00:11:26,421 --> 00:11:28,860
and he vowed to.

130
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:40,320
It was 1964, and something strange was going on in the central part of Florida.

131
00:11:41,280 --> 00:11:46,880
Large parcels of useless swampland near the tiny town of Orlando were being bought up,

132
00:11:47,300 --> 00:11:50,520
but nobody knew why or by whom.

133
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:53,720
There was a lot of speculation in the central Florida area

134
00:11:53,721 --> 00:11:56,620
as to who was purchasing that much land, why would they be doing it.

135
00:11:57,480 --> 00:11:59,160
Floridians may have been in the dark,

136
00:11:59,980 --> 00:12:04,260
but out in California, Walt Disney knew exactly what was going on.

137
00:12:05,080 --> 00:12:08,580
In a cloak-and-dagger mission rivaling the best spy novels,

138
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:13,660
he was acquiring land for a massive new theme park that would dwarf Disneyland.

139
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:17,480
Walt Disney's name was pretty well known to the general public,

140
00:12:17,481 --> 00:12:22,120
and certainly if it had been released that Walt was purchasing that much property here in Florida,

141
00:12:22,121 --> 00:12:23,860
well, that's certainly going to change the price tag.

142
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:28,180
Walt chose Orlando because it offered year-round good weather,

143
00:12:28,660 --> 00:12:30,340
easy access to major roads,

144
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:35,040
and thousands of acres of land at only $180 an acre.

145
00:12:36,240 --> 00:12:41,560
Using false company names like Tomahawk Properties and Latin American Development,

146
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:45,640
Walt and his representatives shuttled in and out of Central Florida,

147
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:47,660
carefully covering their tracks.

148
00:12:48,280 --> 00:12:51,780
We hired attorney Bob Foster to, not under the Disney name,

149
00:12:51,781 --> 00:12:54,274
come down and acquire much of the land.

150
00:12:54,275 --> 00:12:57,280
Bob would always return to California traveling

151
00:12:57,281 --> 00:13:03,520
through different airports along the way. Finally, in the fall of 1965, the Orlando

152
00:13:03,521 --> 00:13:09,439
newspaper got the scoop. We Say It's Disney screamed a huge headline.

153
00:13:09,440 --> 00:13:11,240
Neighboring swampland

154
00:13:11,241 --> 00:13:15,712
immediately shot up to $1,000 an acre.

155
00:13:15,713 --> 00:13:21,360
The Florida Project, all 27,443 acres of it, was

156
00:13:21,361 --> 00:13:25,360
out in the open. At a hastily arranged press conference

157
00:13:25,361 --> 00:13:29,460
along with the governor of Florida, Walt announced the huge undertaking.

158
00:13:29,461 --> 00:13:33,660
He wasn't just building an East Coast Disneyland,

159
00:13:33,661 --> 00:13:37,460
he was creating an entire Disney World,

160
00:13:37,461 --> 00:13:41,460
his most ambitious and technologically innovative enterprise

161
00:13:41,461 --> 00:13:48,460
ever. But the project almost died before the first shovelful of Earth was turned.

162
00:13:48,461 --> 00:13:57,460
Coming up, Walt's dream is put on hold while the Imagineers grapple with whether to press on in the face of an unforeseen crisis.

163
00:13:58,460 --> 00:14:06,460
Due to the success of Disneyland and Walt Disney World, more Disney destinations were built in Paris, Tokyo and Hong Kong.

164
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:10,460
Modern Marvel's Walt Disney World will return in a moment.

165
00:14:18,460 --> 00:14:28,340
In 1966, Walt Disney launched the biggest project of his career,

166
00:14:28,920 --> 00:14:33,760
a massive theme park situated in the middle of 27,000 acres in central Florida.

167
00:14:34,260 --> 00:14:39,220
What you can see on this master plan, the theme park and all the other tourist facilities

168
00:14:39,221 --> 00:14:43,200
fill just one small area of our enormous Florida project.

169
00:14:43,201 --> 00:14:48,580
Walt envisioned several theme parks, hotels, golf courses and thousands of

170
00:14:48,581 --> 00:14:53,440
acres left in their natural state. His dreams were every bit as big as the

171
00:14:53,441 --> 00:14:59,980
acreage he had purchased but he'd never see these dreams completed. On December

172
00:14:59,981 --> 00:15:06,080
15th 1966 Walt Disney died from cancer.

173
00:15:06,081 --> 00:15:12,140
The first few months after Walt died it was sort of like well Walt's just

174
00:15:12,141 --> 00:15:18,960
Walt's just away for a while.

175
00:15:19,640 --> 00:15:23,720
Walt will be back from a vacation, and then we'll get on with the next things that we're going to do.

176
00:15:24,460 --> 00:15:26,660
The Disney company was devastated.

177
00:15:27,760 --> 00:15:32,260
Without their charismatic leader, many felt Disney World couldn't be built.

178
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:36,413
It took us about a year to convince Roy, O.

179
00:15:36,414 --> 00:15:41,300
Disney, and the rest of the management of the Walt Disney Company

180
00:15:41,301 --> 00:15:46,200
that there was a lot of talent here that had worked with Walt Disney for many years

181
00:15:46,201 --> 00:15:48,280
and that we could do this project.

182
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:54,140
Finally, in 1967, Roy Disney, Walt's brother and business partner,

183
00:15:54,520 --> 00:15:56,460
decided to move ahead with Disney World.

184
00:15:57,460 --> 00:16:00,920
The first project would be a theme park modeled after Disneyland,

185
00:16:01,280 --> 00:16:02,840
but more than twice as large.

186
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:06,180
It would be called the Magic Kingdom.

187
00:16:06,181 --> 00:16:18,600
construction officially began in April of 1969 but the Magic Kingdom didn't happen with a wave

188
00:16:18,601 --> 00:16:26,220
of the wand it was more like a military conquest in fact the battle to build Walt Disney World

189
00:16:26,221 --> 00:16:33,420
and the Magic Kingdom was commanded by a Navy Admiral and an Army General General Joe Potter

190
00:16:33,421 --> 00:16:38,780
had overseen the operation of the Panama Canal and the 1964 World's Fair.

191
00:16:39,500 --> 00:16:43,620
And Admiral Joe Fowler had built Disneyland for Walt a decade before.

192
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:45,980
They were perfect for the job.

193
00:16:46,480 --> 00:16:49,460
Joe Fowler was especially perfect from a construction standpoint

194
00:16:49,461 --> 00:16:53,960
because he understood how to work with the Imagineers

195
00:16:53,961 --> 00:16:56,960
and all the creative talent and all the engineers.

196
00:16:57,460 --> 00:17:01,740
Their first challenge was to convert hundreds of acres of swampy land

197
00:17:01,741 --> 00:17:04,800
into rolling hills and sparkling waterways.

198
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:08,340
They started by digging into Bay Lake,

199
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:12,020
400 acres of reeds, muck, and swamp grass

200
00:17:12,021 --> 00:17:14,760
lined with 14 feet of rotted roots.

201
00:17:15,780 --> 00:17:18,660
They drained the lake and went down

202
00:17:18,661 --> 00:17:23,280
and dug out a good layer of muck and mire

203
00:17:23,281 --> 00:17:24,740
that was down at the lake bottom.

204
00:17:25,580 --> 00:17:29,380
After 18 months of moving more than 8 million cubic yards of dirt,

205
00:17:29,381 --> 00:17:34,380
the largest private construction project in the country, they were ready to pour concrete.

206
00:17:35,400 --> 00:17:39,940
But the concrete work wasn't for any of the landmark structures visitors today would recognize.

207
00:17:40,840 --> 00:17:45,180
In fact, it wasn't for structures any guests were ever supposed to see.

208
00:17:47,460 --> 00:17:50,100
Back during the early days of Disneyland,

209
00:17:50,840 --> 00:17:55,160
Walt once saw a Frontierland cowboy sauntering through Tomorrowland,

210
00:17:55,161 --> 00:17:58,420
which utterly destroyed the futuristic sci-fi mood.

211
00:17:59,380 --> 00:18:02,500
To avoid that kind of culture clash at Walt Disney World,

212
00:18:02,960 --> 00:18:06,320
the Imagineers designed a network of tunnels to service the park.

213
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:10,240
They were called Utilidors, Utility Corridors.

214
00:18:11,520 --> 00:18:15,840
Because the water table in central Florida is only two feet below ground level,

215
00:18:16,300 --> 00:18:20,640
the Imagineers built the Utilidors on top of the ground rather than under it.

216
00:18:21,160 --> 00:18:24,860
They are on ground level. They are the first floor of the Magic Kingdom.

217
00:18:24,861 --> 00:18:30,620
Then the Magic Kingdom Park was built on the second and third stories on top of that.

218
00:18:31,640 --> 00:18:38,080
The Magic Kingdom is actually 14 feet above where the original elevation was.

219
00:18:38,980 --> 00:18:45,860
The Utilidors made it possible to walk from one end of the park to the other in just 10 minutes without being seen by guests.

220
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:52,240
The Utilidors also had many of the mundane operations that could break the Disney magic.

221
00:18:52,241 --> 00:18:58,340
They have office buildings, facilities, wardrobe, cafeteria.

222
00:18:58,341 --> 00:19:03,660
The tunnels also housed an underground trash collection system, one of the world's biggest

223
00:19:03,661 --> 00:19:05,380
vacuum cleaners.

224
00:19:05,381 --> 00:19:12,220
Our engineers found the Swedish system, the AVAC system, which we incorporated to collect

225
00:19:12,221 --> 00:19:13,840
trash pneumatically.

226
00:19:13,841 --> 00:19:20,240
You know, like those old department store things where the cashiers would take something

227
00:19:20,241 --> 00:19:22,040
and put it in a tube and send it off.

228
00:19:22,660 --> 00:19:24,360
Basically, it works the same way,

229
00:19:24,440 --> 00:19:29,500
and the trash goes from specified points to a central collection area.

230
00:19:30,200 --> 00:19:34,540
With the site prep and underground foundations laid by General Potter's army,

231
00:19:35,080 --> 00:19:38,420
Admiral Joe Fowler was ready to start building the park itself.

232
00:19:38,860 --> 00:19:42,040
His biggest challenge would be what Walt had envisioned

233
00:19:42,041 --> 00:19:44,580
as the Magic Kingdom's crowning structure,

234
00:19:45,080 --> 00:19:47,100
a towering Cinderella castle.

235
00:19:47,101 --> 00:19:55,940
But the castle couldn't be too tall, because Florida law required a flashing red light on any structure higher than 200 feet.

236
00:19:57,360 --> 00:20:03,720
Fowler's contractors ordered 600 tons of structural steel and began assembling the castle skeleton.

237
00:20:04,500 --> 00:20:09,460
Day by day, the girders rose, hoisted into the Florida sky by giant cranes.

238
00:20:10,200 --> 00:20:14,300
To speed the progress, they prefabricated sections of the castle.

239
00:20:14,301 --> 00:20:21,800
not just steel framework but complete towers and turrets 18 at all while on

240
00:20:21,801 --> 00:20:25,460
the ground they were sheathed in fiberglass panels molded to look like

241
00:20:25,461 --> 00:20:33,200
stone real gold top three of the towers the highest tower of Cinderella Castle

242
00:20:33,201 --> 00:20:37,880
was hoisted and fastened in place 18 months after it was begun in July of

243
00:20:37,881 --> 00:20:38,581
1971.

244
00:20:38,582 --> 00:20:46,620
The castle topped out at 189 feet, just below Florida's height limit of 200 feet.

245
00:20:47,640 --> 00:20:52,280
But Disney's Imagineers wanted the Magic Kingdom's castle to look much higher,

246
00:20:52,620 --> 00:20:56,520
so they worked a little magic of their own, optical magic.

247
00:20:57,500 --> 00:21:01,800
First perspective is a technique that's been used in filmmaking for many, many years,

248
00:21:01,801 --> 00:21:07,400
and it's where you'll take a building and you will scale it differently up close to you.

249
00:21:07,401 --> 00:21:14,740
So if you've got a building that's maybe several stories tall, then the windows and the framework will get smaller and smaller.

250
00:21:15,140 --> 00:21:20,020
That gives the illusion that the structure is actually bigger than it really is or further away than it may actually be.

251
00:21:21,260 --> 00:21:31,160
On October 1st, 1971, Roy Disney unveiled Cinderella Castle and the rest of the Magic Kingdom to a crowd of 10,000 people.

252
00:21:31,161 --> 00:21:41,160
Walt Disney World is a tribute to the philosophy and the life of Walter Elias Disney.

253
00:21:41,161 --> 00:21:48,160
Unlike Disneyland's opening 15 years earlier, the event ran like clockwork, proof of both

254
00:21:48,161 --> 00:21:52,160
Walt's vision and the Imagineers' technical achievements.

255
00:21:52,161 --> 00:21:58,160
But the greatest achievement wasn't creating the park, the tunnels or even the castle.

256
00:21:58,161 --> 00:22:06,160
It was an array of high-tech figures designed by Walt himself 10 years earlier.

257
00:22:06,161 --> 00:22:13,160
Disney's contemporary resort was built with modular construction.

258
00:22:13,161 --> 00:22:17,160
Nine-ton rooms were finished out with built-in furniture three miles away,

259
00:22:17,161 --> 00:22:21,160
then trucked to the site and slid into place like dresser drawers.

260
00:22:21,161 --> 00:22:25,160
Modern Marvel's Walt Disney World will return in a moment.

261
00:22:28,160 --> 00:22:40,560
When Walt Disney World opened in 1971, it seemed like the entire East Coast funneled

262
00:22:40,561 --> 00:22:43,340
straight into the theme park.

263
00:22:43,341 --> 00:22:47,340
Attendance hit 10 million in less than two years.

264
00:22:47,341 --> 00:22:52,200
People came by the carload to see popular attractions like Jungle Cruise, It's a Small

265
00:22:52,201 --> 00:22:55,000
World and the Hall of Presidents.

266
00:22:55,001 --> 00:22:59,600
Like their counterparts at Disneyland, they all had one thing in common.

267
00:23:00,640 --> 00:23:04,080
They featured what many people consider Walt's greatest legacy.

268
00:23:05,460 --> 00:23:08,620
The robotic figures he called audio animatronics.

269
00:23:09,620 --> 00:23:14,360
And they owed their existence to the 1964 New York World's Fair.

270
00:23:18,320 --> 00:23:23,840
Back in 1961, when Walt Disney heard that a World's Fair was being planned for New York City,

271
00:23:23,841 --> 00:23:28,240
he contracted with several major companies to create rides for the fair.

272
00:23:29,360 --> 00:23:31,700
Walt wanted to be able to develop this,

273
00:23:31,920 --> 00:23:35,380
and it was a way to get the sponsorship and some of the money

274
00:23:35,381 --> 00:23:37,140
to help develop the ideas that he had.

275
00:23:37,620 --> 00:23:41,380
One idea he'd been working on for years was robotic figures,

276
00:23:41,660 --> 00:23:44,760
which he called audio animatronics, or AAs.

277
00:23:45,320 --> 00:23:49,320
He had visions the way he could build a mechanical electrical machine

278
00:23:49,321 --> 00:23:52,760
and then turn it into a life form that looked realistic

279
00:23:52,761 --> 00:23:56,300
and moved you out of that illusion of, well, it's not a machine,

280
00:23:56,460 --> 00:23:59,560
but it's got character and it's real and it comes alive.

281
00:24:00,380 --> 00:24:03,200
Walt had built simple pneumatic AA figures

282
00:24:03,201 --> 00:24:05,900
into the Enchanted Tiki Room show at Disneyland.

283
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,660
What made it so unique was all the surprises.

284
00:24:14,900 --> 00:24:17,440
You know, that you'd be sitting there and all of a sudden the drums would come

285
00:24:17,441 --> 00:24:20,160
and the tiki poles would start to animate.

286
00:24:20,161 --> 00:24:23,560
And then of course the finale when the orchid's up and the stuff starts singing.

287
00:24:23,561 --> 00:24:28,100
Everything in that theater comes to life.

288
00:24:28,101 --> 00:24:31,280
And that was very similar to what he used to do in his animated films,

289
00:24:31,281 --> 00:24:34,360
where flowers would sing and birds would talk, etc.

290
00:24:34,500 --> 00:24:35,740
And buildings would come to life.

291
00:24:36,780 --> 00:24:38,140
But Walt wanted more.

292
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,220
He wanted lifelike human AAs.

293
00:24:42,020 --> 00:24:46,760
For years, his Imagineers had been struggling to create an audio animatronic

294
00:24:46,761 --> 00:24:49,860
of Walt's favorite president, Abraham Lincoln.

295
00:24:50,160 --> 00:24:59,920
We wanted to bring to the people of today the inspiring words of the man who held this nation together during its moment of greatest crisis, the Civil War.

296
00:25:00,800 --> 00:25:08,140
When Walt started pitching ideas to the World's Fair, one of the World's Fair officials, Robert Moses, was fascinated.

297
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:11,600
Robert Moses said, I won't open the fair without Mr. Lincoln.

298
00:25:12,200 --> 00:25:16,220
The trouble was, Lincoln was nowhere close to being operational.

299
00:25:17,080 --> 00:25:21,100
The simple system that ran the Tiki Birds wasn't advanced enough for Lincoln.

300
00:25:21,920 --> 00:25:25,620
To make him seem human, he would need a far more complex mechanism.

301
00:25:26,300 --> 00:25:29,340
And it would have to be packed into a very small space.

302
00:25:30,560 --> 00:25:32,220
Lincoln's skull, to be precise.

303
00:25:33,620 --> 00:25:37,780
As the fair drew closer and closer, Lincoln still wasn't ready.

304
00:25:38,720 --> 00:25:41,740
Walt was very, very upset with it and told me, he says,

305
00:25:41,741 --> 00:25:45,640
Bobby, I want twice as many motions and half as much weight.

306
00:25:46,220 --> 00:25:50,240
not having ever designed humans or animals, it was like a whole new thing,

307
00:25:50,300 --> 00:25:52,100
but which we're used to doing whole new things.

308
00:25:52,540 --> 00:25:57,700
I immediately saw it as an airplane fuselage with small bearings, small steel framework,

309
00:25:58,340 --> 00:26:01,340
and in 90 days I laid out the entire design.

310
00:26:02,140 --> 00:26:06,440
Lincoln was given an internal framework with 13 main structural units,

311
00:26:06,441 --> 00:26:08,420
all moving in dozens of directions.

312
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:13,260
The articulated motions were powered by large pneumatic servo cylinders.

313
00:26:13,261 --> 00:26:22,260
early tests proved the design could work still there were problems Walt was pulling out some

314
00:26:22,261 --> 00:26:26,640
of the dignitaries from state of Illinois to where they could come out and preview what mr.

315
00:26:26,641 --> 00:26:28,465
Lincoln was going to look like mr.

316
00:26:28,466 --> 00:26:31,740
Lincoln stood up and he started giving his speech and at that

317
00:26:31,741 --> 00:26:37,800
time and frame our hydraulic fluids were red in color unfortunately when he was doing his show

318
00:26:37,801 --> 00:26:40,600
we blew a hydraulic line in the torso of Mr.

319
00:26:40,601 --> 00:26:43,400
Lincoln and all of a sudden he collapsed and

320
00:26:43,401 --> 00:26:46,645
we had this red dye fluid started to leak through his white shirt.

321
00:26:46,646 --> 00:26:48,120
Some of the dignitaries in the

322
00:26:48,121 --> 00:26:51,239
audience said, oh you've recreated the assassination of Mr.

323
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:53,460
Lincoln and Walt wasn't very pleased about

324
00:26:53,461 --> 00:26:57,200
that so from that point on we had to use clear hydraulic fluid.

325
00:26:57,201 --> 00:26:59,100
He made his debut at the World's

326
00:26:59,101 --> 00:27:02,320
Fair in April of 1964 to a stunned audience.

327
00:27:02,321 --> 00:27:05,760
There were reports at the New York World's Fair

328
00:27:05,761 --> 00:27:08,480
that Mr. Lincoln got up off the stage and shook hands with people.

329
00:27:09,100 --> 00:27:12,100
But from the Imagineers that I talked to who were there, that never happened.

330
00:27:12,760 --> 00:27:16,020
And he essentially was bolted to the floor for the duration of his run.

331
00:27:16,260 --> 00:27:20,400
Just resonated with people in a way that left them very enthusiastic,

332
00:27:20,401 --> 00:27:25,780
not only about Mr. Lincoln, but about seeing entertainment robotics in general.

333
00:27:26,520 --> 00:27:28,600
Now that they could make human figures,

334
00:27:28,980 --> 00:27:31,960
Disney's Imagineers created hundreds of AAs.

335
00:27:33,020 --> 00:27:35,680
They were traditionally hydraulic or pneumatic figures.

336
00:27:35,760 --> 00:27:40,980
This means that they have either fluid going through tubes at high pressure, that's hydraulic,

337
00:27:41,580 --> 00:27:44,380
or pneumatic is air going through tubes at varying pressure.

338
00:27:45,240 --> 00:27:50,020
As the air or fluid flowed at varying pressures, it triggered the actuators,

339
00:27:50,021 --> 00:27:52,820
the mechanisms that caused the automatic motions.

340
00:27:53,960 --> 00:27:58,200
We have what we call rotax, which is short for rotary actuators.

341
00:27:58,680 --> 00:28:02,980
They do a rotary motion of a wrist twist or torso twist,

342
00:28:02,981 --> 00:28:07,980
Then we have linear actuators that will do a linear motion.

343
00:28:07,981 --> 00:28:14,980
Actuators power even the tiny facial movements that make AA's so convincing.

344
00:28:14,981 --> 00:28:19,980
We have an actuator that we call the pinch actuator, and it pinches the corner of the lips.

345
00:28:19,981 --> 00:28:23,980
When you pronounce certain words, the corners of your mouth will pinch.

346
00:28:23,981 --> 00:28:26,980
And then the actuators down off the side is what we call the smile actuators.

347
00:28:26,981 --> 00:28:31,460
actuators, so when those actuators move up and down, they actually pull the lips back

348
00:28:31,461 --> 00:28:35,060
and give the look of the figure smiling.

349
00:28:35,061 --> 00:28:39,920
Up at the eyes, we have an actuator that can either be digital or analog, that will give

350
00:28:39,921 --> 00:28:42,200
you eyes right or left.

351
00:28:42,201 --> 00:28:45,320
If they're digital, they're spring-loaded and they will come back to center each time.

352
00:28:45,321 --> 00:28:50,600
If they're analog, they will go from point A to B, and we can stop them and control them

353
00:28:50,601 --> 00:28:53,600
any range and point we want.

354
00:28:53,601 --> 00:28:58,840
Figure control and thus greater realism was always the goal for AAs.

355
00:28:58,841 --> 00:29:03,960
But in the early days of Disney World, that realism required a trade-off in speed.

356
00:29:03,961 --> 00:29:09,440
If we wanted to move the arm swing real fast, what would happen was the figure would stop

357
00:29:09,441 --> 00:29:12,600
and then it would shake and resonate and go all the way through the whole figure.

358
00:29:12,601 --> 00:29:15,640
It looked very unnatural.

359
00:29:15,641 --> 00:29:20,240
So what we would do is we had to slow that function down to where it looked fluid and

360
00:29:20,241 --> 00:29:22,860
more natural and not resonate and shake.

361
00:29:22,861 --> 00:29:27,940
So we always had to sacrifice speed for control, which drove the animators crazy because they

362
00:29:27,941 --> 00:29:30,820
wanted speed and still have control.

363
00:29:30,821 --> 00:29:36,040
The Imagineers created a breakthrough in audio animatronics in the 1980s.

364
00:29:36,041 --> 00:29:41,360
By adding a new circuit called compliance, they were able to control the amount of force

365
00:29:41,361 --> 00:29:44,080
pushing or pulling on a movement.

366
00:29:44,081 --> 00:29:48,760
We have built-in compliance in the human body to where when my brain says stop, maybe the

367
00:29:48,761 --> 00:29:52,360
inertia makes my arm overshoot a little bit and then comes back to where I told

368
00:29:52,361 --> 00:29:56,680
it to stop, you know, but it looked very natural because that's how the human

369
00:29:56,681 --> 00:30:01,300
body works. We did the same thing with an electronic circuit and either pressure

370
00:30:01,301 --> 00:30:06,520
transducers or mechanical Hall effect sensor. We can get that fast speed now

371
00:30:06,521 --> 00:30:10,960
and then it will naturally overshoot and come back that looks very realistic and

372
00:30:10,961 --> 00:30:15,700
you know fluid like the human body. So with that new technology we're able to

373
00:30:15,701 --> 00:30:19,940
make figures almost look just like us, sometimes even better.

374
00:30:21,620 --> 00:30:26,040
Today, the Magic Kingdom's AAs populate dozens of attractions.

375
00:30:26,920 --> 00:30:31,520
From shows like the Hall of Presidents, where Abe Lincoln still stands and talks.

376
00:30:31,740 --> 00:30:35,020
For a thousand generations, rejoice.

377
00:30:35,380 --> 00:30:37,380
To adventures like the Jungle Cruise.

378
00:30:38,180 --> 00:30:41,620
Each attraction is a precision-timed combination of soundtrack,

379
00:30:42,180 --> 00:30:44,560
which includes music, dialogue and sound effects.

380
00:30:44,561 --> 00:30:46,560
and AA motions.

381
00:30:48,260 --> 00:30:54,620
The complex animated characters are controlled by the Digital Animation Control System, or DAX.

382
00:30:55,320 --> 00:30:59,400
It's located in climate-controlled rooms in the tunnels beneath the Magic Kingdom.

383
00:31:00,420 --> 00:31:04,340
Electrical cables that run through the tunnels connect the programs to the shows,

384
00:31:04,800 --> 00:31:07,820
enabling technicians to run the shows from a remote location.

385
00:31:08,960 --> 00:31:14,140
Each one of these is a flash memory card that contains all of that data,

386
00:31:14,560 --> 00:31:18,960
in a non-moving and non-volatile format, so there are no moving parts.

387
00:31:19,960 --> 00:31:23,600
As the chip plays, it hands off to the next one,

388
00:31:23,880 --> 00:31:27,680
and then hands off to the next one and so forth, until we have a complete message.

389
00:31:28,420 --> 00:31:36,160
We're able to patch into the cards to monitor their performance.

390
00:31:36,161 --> 00:31:39,660
Wrap one a table. It's time to respond.

391
00:31:40,840 --> 00:31:44,400
Send us a message from somewhere beyond.

392
00:31:46,620 --> 00:31:51,720
Today, a small army of audio-animatronic characters populates the Magic Kingdom.

393
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:53,680
Some 1,100 in all.

394
00:31:54,480 --> 00:31:59,160
It's the kind of creativity and technology Disney's Imagineers would put to work,

395
00:31:59,400 --> 00:32:01,820
yet again, when they tackled their next part.

396
00:32:01,821 --> 00:32:06,520
Coming up, building the world's first geodesic sphere

397
00:32:06,521 --> 00:32:11,360
and the technology behind Disney's cutting-edge fireworks displays.

398
00:32:15,040 --> 00:32:19,760
Disney's audio-animatronic figure Lucky the Dinosaur has traveled the globe,

399
00:32:20,040 --> 00:32:22,980
making visits to California, Orlando, and Hong Kong.

400
00:32:23,560 --> 00:32:26,680
Modern Marvels. Walt Disney World will return in a moment.

401
00:32:31,820 --> 00:32:43,340
EPCOT is a 300-acre park with two very distinct personalities.

402
00:32:44,620 --> 00:32:49,960
One side is a futuristic world dominated by the 180-foot-tall spaceship Earth,

403
00:32:50,380 --> 00:32:53,580
one of the biggest geospheres anywhere on our geosphere.

404
00:32:55,980 --> 00:33:01,340
At the other end of the park, 11 foreign pavilions ring the World Showcase Lagoon.

405
00:33:01,820 --> 00:33:06,820
Given Disney's history with World's Fairs, it's no coincidence.

406
00:33:06,821 --> 00:33:10,820
Epcot is a permanent World's Fair, if you will.

407
00:33:13,820 --> 00:33:17,820
When Disney Imagineers broke ground on Epcot in 1979,

408
00:33:17,821 --> 00:33:22,820
they were once again launching the biggest construction project in the United States.

409
00:33:22,821 --> 00:33:28,820
3,000 designers, 22 construction companies, 500 subcontractors,

410
00:33:28,821 --> 00:33:32,920
subcontractors and more than 10,000 workers took on the job.

411
00:33:35,520 --> 00:33:41,240
Much of their labor went into constructing Spaceship Earth, a ride inside a giant sphere

412
00:33:41,241 --> 00:33:46,000
that was much more challenging to build than the castles that anchored Disney's other parks.

413
00:33:47,060 --> 00:33:54,500
Epcot was a world theme, and we wanted to somehow express that. And so a sphere,

414
00:33:54,501 --> 00:34:01,080
you know, looks like the globe of the world, much more so than a golf ball as it's been called from time to time.

415
00:34:01,840 --> 00:34:05,600
But it's a very fascinating, complicated building.

416
00:34:06,660 --> 00:34:09,960
Spaceship Earth is the world's first geodesic sphere.

417
00:34:11,280 --> 00:34:18,920
Its outside surface is 150,000 square feet, and its inside volume is 2,200,000 cubic feet.

418
00:34:18,921 --> 00:34:27,780
The giant sphere is held up by six legs, each of which is supported by pylons driven 120 to 185 feet into the ground.

419
00:34:28,520 --> 00:34:32,300
At its lowest point, the sphere stands 18 feet off the ground.

420
00:34:32,960 --> 00:34:45,480
We actually hired MIT to engineer and test the wind loads to make sure that it wouldn't become underneath there a wind tunnel that would knock people over.

421
00:34:45,481 --> 00:34:52,480
The sphere is made of more than 11,000 triangles of a Leuka bond, a thermal polyethylene core bonded to aluminum.

422
00:34:52,481 --> 00:34:58,480
One inch spacing between the panels allows them to expand and contract in the Florida heat.

423
00:34:58,481 --> 00:35:04,480
The spacing also prevents the sphere from becoming a huge waterfall when it rains.

424
00:35:04,481 --> 00:35:11,480
Water flows between the panels into a gutter system that empties into the World Showcase Lagoon.

425
00:35:11,481 --> 00:35:18,480
But Spaceship Earth is hardly the only engineering marvel in Epcot.

426
00:35:18,481 --> 00:35:24,380
Connecting Epcot to the Magic Kingdom is Disney's most famous and futuristic mode of transportation,

427
00:35:24,381 --> 00:35:25,380
the monorail.

428
00:35:25,381 --> 00:35:36,260
People still look to the monorail as sort of a vision of future transportation.

429
00:35:36,261 --> 00:35:41,780
a bus on a highway and it had wheels on the bottom then you put that up on a

430
00:35:41,781 --> 00:35:46,280
concrete road in the sky and maybe it's two feet wide so you take some other

431
00:35:46,281 --> 00:35:50,500
wheels and you put them on the side of this concrete road that keeps it upright

432
00:35:50,501 --> 00:35:56,060
Disney didn't invent the monorail it was first patented in the 1820s in Great

433
00:35:56,061 --> 00:36:02,700
Britain but Walt gave the monorail a futuristic makeover you remember the

434
00:36:02,701 --> 00:36:06,300
Buck Rogers rocket ship where it had a long pointed front.

435
00:36:06,440 --> 00:36:10,060
It had these splayed fins along the side like ski runners.

436
00:36:10,520 --> 00:36:14,140
But it had this big wraparound windshield and little portholes.

437
00:36:14,240 --> 00:36:15,860
And I thought, that's it.

438
00:36:15,980 --> 00:36:18,820
That's all we got to do is do that shape and it'll hide the wheels.

439
00:36:19,460 --> 00:36:20,540
Sketched it up real quick.

440
00:36:20,800 --> 00:36:23,740
Came in, made a bigger drawing of it than a rendering of it.

441
00:36:24,180 --> 00:36:26,600
Walt took one look at the rendering and said, can you build that?

442
00:36:27,180 --> 00:36:28,460
They could indeed.

443
00:36:28,461 --> 00:36:38,460
The original Disneyland monorail, which gave its first ride to Vice President Richard Nixon in 1959, ran on a track only 4,200 feet long.

444
00:36:38,461 --> 00:36:41,460
More a carnival ride than a transportation system.

445
00:36:41,461 --> 00:36:49,460
For Walt Disney World, though, Imagineers scaled up the monorail to provide serious transportation.

446
00:36:49,461 --> 00:36:57,680
Walt Disney World, we needed a longer train, so we took basically what I call the original

447
00:36:57,681 --> 00:37:03,520
Mark III design that we put into service here in 1968, and we made it wider and we made

448
00:37:03,521 --> 00:37:08,720
it longer, and I designed a new body to go on that train that had this sort of Learjet

449
00:37:08,721 --> 00:37:11,900
sweeping windshield look to it.

450
00:37:11,901 --> 00:37:15,380
