00:55:17,981 --> 00:55:26,980
The Rock and Roller coaster is to conventional coasters what an F-18 Hornet is to a single engine prop plane.

673
00:55:26,981 --> 00:55:34,980
Instead of slowly hauling its way up a long, slow climb, this coaster takes off like a rocket.

674
00:55:34,981 --> 00:55:42,980
It launches guests in the Super Stretch limo from zero to 60 in under three seconds.

675
00:55:44,980 --> 00:55:47,520
And you're immediately going into an inversion.

676
00:55:48,380 --> 00:55:49,540
We've got some loops.

677
00:55:49,820 --> 00:55:51,780
We've got some surprises along the dark.

678
00:55:51,880 --> 00:55:54,160
At the same time, you're seeing landmarks zipping by.

679
00:55:55,560 --> 00:55:56,640
It's about speed.

680
00:55:56,740 --> 00:55:57,520
It's about thrill.

681
00:55:57,820 --> 00:55:58,640
It's about music.

682
00:55:58,780 --> 00:56:00,580
It's combining all three into one experience.

683
00:56:00,581 --> 00:56:07,580
The Rockin' Roller Coaster's launch system is actually a series of powerful electromagnets,

684
00:56:07,581 --> 00:56:12,580
synchronized to pull the car forward at ever-increasing speeds.

685
00:56:12,581 --> 00:56:22,580
Called Linear Synchronous Motors, the technology is now being explored by the Navy as a better way to catapult planes off of aircraft carriers

686
00:56:22,581 --> 00:56:28,580
and by NASA as a more efficient way to launch space vehicles.

687
00:56:28,581 --> 00:56:34,720
Surprisingly, this high-tech launch technology is descended from a transportation system

688
00:56:34,721 --> 00:56:41,060
first unveiled by Walt Disney at the New York World's Fair and later upgraded for the Magic

689
00:56:41,061 --> 00:56:42,060
Kingdom.

690
00:56:42,061 --> 00:56:47,200
We designed and built for Walt Disney World at PeopleMover that was silent running and

691
00:56:47,201 --> 00:56:52,940
had no moving parts because the track had embedded in it a series of magnets so that

692
00:56:52,941 --> 00:56:58,100
as each car would come across these magnets, the power would turn on and off and just urge

693
00:56:58,101 --> 00:57:01,100
the car smoothly along the track.

694
00:57:01,101 --> 00:57:07,400
450 linear induction motors propel the people mover, which is still smoothly running at

695
00:57:07,401 --> 00:57:12,640
Walt Disney World at a stately 7 miles an hour.

696
00:57:12,641 --> 00:57:19,100
Enter the turbocharged cousin of that technology called linear synchronous motors, or LSMs.

697
00:57:19,101 --> 00:57:24,840
LSMs accelerate the firing of the electromagnets along the track, turning a plodding magnetic

698
00:57:24,841 --> 00:57:27,940
ripple into a screaming tsunami.

699
00:57:27,941 --> 00:57:34,040
And anything surfing that tsunami can be accelerated to a speed limited only by the bursts of electrical current

700
00:57:34,041 --> 00:57:37,680
and the physical limits of ride vehicles and their occupants.

701
00:57:38,520 --> 00:57:41,300
There's so much power you can get out of a linear motor.

702
00:57:41,680 --> 00:57:47,640
With a linear motor, you can literally be magnetically squirted out into space with no chain or anything.

703
00:57:48,100 --> 00:57:50,000
That's the big advantage of linear motor.

704
00:57:51,120 --> 00:57:55,320
Whether you're in the first car or the last, the songs match the scenery.

705
00:57:55,321 --> 00:58:01,380
Each onboard audio system also has unique sensors on the ride vehicles and they correspond

706
00:58:01,381 --> 00:58:08,000
with cues that are given along the ride path and that tells the system what tracks to play.

707
00:58:08,001 --> 00:58:13,400
It's a pretty complex matrix of amplifiers and speakers and crossovers.

708
00:58:13,401 --> 00:58:16,000
We're separating highs, mid-ranges and lows.

709
00:58:16,001 --> 00:58:19,280
You can tell that there are speakers right beside your head, almost like an earphone,

710
00:58:19,281 --> 00:58:23,120
and there's subwoofers underneath your seat, so you're really immersed in this audio experience.

711
00:58:25,320 --> 00:58:31,320
Not all the vehicles at Walt Disney World are attached to a track.

712
00:58:31,321 --> 00:58:37,980
Coming up, Disney's Imagineers attempt to harness the kinetic energy and unpredictable

713
00:58:37,981 --> 00:58:44,700
thrills of a Hollywood car chase.

714
00:58:44,701 --> 00:58:49,340
Producer Jack Douglas scored the music for the rock and roller coaster by riding it like

715
00:58:49,341 --> 00:58:54,240
a hundred times with a computer strapped to his knees.

716
00:58:54,241 --> 00:58:57,200
Marvel's Walt Disney World will return in a moment.

717
00:59:06,560 --> 00:59:11,060
When the Disney Imagineers were planning the Disney MGM Studios theme park,

718
00:59:11,400 --> 00:59:14,280
they knew they wanted to stage some sort of stunt show.

719
00:59:15,460 --> 00:59:17,040
But the question was, how?

720
00:59:18,180 --> 00:59:22,320
They had plenty of experience designing and building things like roller coasters

721
00:59:22,321 --> 00:59:24,020
and monorails and animatronics,

722
00:59:24,360 --> 00:59:27,760
but those were all pre-programmed and highly controlled.

723
00:59:28,580 --> 00:59:31,700
How could they bring that level of control and precision

724
00:59:31,701 --> 00:59:35,300
to something as kinetic and unpredictable as a car chase?

725
00:59:39,640 --> 00:59:43,260
How do we make this something that can perform day in and day out

726
00:59:43,261 --> 00:59:44,400
and be very consistent?

727
00:59:45,460 --> 00:59:47,500
And inventing and building the technology

728
00:59:47,501 --> 00:59:50,860
to make such an attraction possible was only the beginning.

729
00:59:50,861 --> 00:59:55,540
A lot of science went into designing the way we're going to stage everything.

730
00:59:56,380 --> 00:59:59,440
Creating the show began with finding the right location.

731
00:59:59,960 --> 01:00:04,180
In this case, a five-acre corner of the park that was ripe for redevelopment.

732
01:00:04,840 --> 01:00:06,420
There was only one problem.

733
01:00:07,820 --> 01:00:09,460
A canal ran through the site.

734
01:00:10,520 --> 01:00:12,660
We had to move the canal to make room for it.

735
01:00:12,940 --> 01:00:18,900
But that kind of obstacle is something that we face probably every time we build something.

736
01:00:18,901 --> 01:00:28,900
Once the engineers redirected the canal, the Imagineers began building a 177,000 square foot arena that included a 5,000 seat stadium.

737
01:00:29,800 --> 01:00:33,900
First, they poured concrete footers four feet deep to support bridges and ramps.

738
01:00:35,760 --> 01:00:41,480
Beneath it all, the Imagineers buried a pit big enough to hold liquid propane.

739
01:00:42,700 --> 01:00:48,640
We do have a pit that all the accumulators are for the LP gas.

740
01:00:48,900 --> 01:00:54,020
which are fed from a fuel farm, which holds propane, which fuel these accumulators.

741
01:00:56,580 --> 01:01:00,500
Most of that firepower would be reserved for the show's planned finale,

742
01:01:00,501 --> 01:01:04,500
a spectacular display of stunts, thrills, and explosions.

743
01:01:08,180 --> 01:01:14,262
We have a finale, which is six large compressed LP gas accumulators that blow off.

744
01:01:14,263 --> 01:01:15,300
They put about

745
01:01:15,301 --> 01:01:19,300
about a 45 to 65 foot fireball in the air for the finale.

746
01:01:21,300 --> 01:01:25,300
But what would a Hollywood car chase be without a gunfight?

747
01:01:25,301 --> 01:01:28,300
Obviously, real guns were out of the question,

748
01:01:28,301 --> 01:01:33,300
so the Imagineers turned to one of their most trusted technologies, pneumatics.

749
01:01:34,300 --> 01:01:37,300
We also have what we call bullet hits, which are pneumatic,

750
01:01:37,301 --> 01:01:39,300
which are compressed air in the set piece,

751
01:01:39,301 --> 01:01:43,300
so that when the villain shoots at the hero,

752
01:01:43,301 --> 01:01:49,800
you see water shooting up out of the ground, which is basically compressed air with a water socket full.

753
01:01:50,840 --> 01:01:55,720
Once the stage was underway, the Imagineers went to work on the ride vehicles.

754
01:01:56,720 --> 01:02:00,680
Although in this case, the vehicles wouldn't be ridden in by visitors.

755
01:02:01,160 --> 01:02:03,040
They'd be driven by stuntmen.

756
01:02:04,260 --> 01:02:09,300
Because of all the jumping and spinning they would have to do, the cars needed to be lightweight.

757
01:02:09,301 --> 01:02:13,800
weight. So the Imagineers took opals and stripped them down to custom chassis.

758
01:02:13,801 --> 01:02:19,980
The goal was a car weighing just over 1,000 pounds, about a third as much as a regular

759
01:02:19,981 --> 01:02:20,681
sedan.

760
01:02:20,682 --> 01:02:25,680
To keep the weight down, but provide the acceleration the cars would need, they

761
01:02:25,681 --> 01:02:32,880
installed a lightweight 1,300cc motorcycle engine that puts out about 152 horsepower.

762
01:02:32,881 --> 01:02:36,760
This engine provides maximum power at a minimum weight.

763
01:02:36,761 --> 01:02:43,540
We have a firewall which protects the driver, as well as we have an external alternator

764
01:02:43,541 --> 01:02:50,260
to charge the system, two large fans to pull air from this compartment out the backside

765
01:02:50,261 --> 01:02:57,220
of the car, and we also cool our oil to the engine to keep it cool to save on clutches.

766
01:02:57,221 --> 01:03:03,180
The engine was put behind the driver to provide greater stability during spins and jumps.

767
01:03:03,181 --> 01:03:08,380
The engine had to be behind the driver's seat to make sure the car landed on all four tires.

768
01:03:09,220 --> 01:03:13,580
For safety, the gas tank was reduced to the smallest possible size,

769
01:03:13,780 --> 01:03:16,600
just big enough to hold a show's worth of gas.

770
01:03:18,500 --> 01:03:20,660
These cars were not built for comfort.

771
01:03:21,720 --> 01:03:24,400
The interior is stripped down much like a race car.

772
01:03:25,000 --> 01:03:30,160
You have a stock car seat, stock car steering wheels, control dash.

773
01:03:30,960 --> 01:03:36,120
There's none of the normal stuff you would have in a car like air condition or radio or any of that stuff that adds weight.

774
01:03:36,380 --> 01:03:38,940
It's just the mechanics that are needed to drive the car.

775
01:03:39,560 --> 01:03:44,080
The Imagineers also designed in some stuff that you wouldn't find in a race car,

776
01:03:44,500 --> 01:03:47,800
like pyrotechnics and specially designed controls and switches.

777
01:03:48,200 --> 01:03:57,260
We have pyro loaded onto the car for the show to give billet hits as well as an effect of a cannon shooting off.

778
01:04:00,160 --> 01:04:08,580
You have a speed limiter switch, which gives you two different speeds in the car for each jump that this car might take.

779
01:04:09,060 --> 01:04:17,940
The transmission was also specially designed and is definitely unlike anything you find in any kind of car, family sedan or Indy racer.

780
01:04:18,340 --> 01:04:22,520
These cars can go up to 60 miles per hour in reverse.

781
01:04:23,680 --> 01:04:25,640
The shifters in this car are bump shifts.

782
01:04:25,641 --> 01:04:30,700
So we have four gears going forward and then four gears going in reverse and you just bump the shifter to shift the gears.

783
01:04:31,340 --> 01:04:35,620
In reverse, we have four gears to give us the torque in the lower end so we can get faster acceleration.

784
01:04:35,980 --> 01:04:38,500
Then we can increase our speed by shifting to higher gears.

785
01:04:39,320 --> 01:04:45,060
The Imagineers also put a specially designed hydraulic brake in the cars called an e-brake.

786
01:04:48,400 --> 01:04:51,100
It's all hydraulic. It doesn't lock when you pull it up.

787
01:04:51,101 --> 01:04:55,560
So you can spin the car around and release it quick enough to go straight again.

788
01:04:55,640 --> 01:05:04,640
In fact, the e-brake makes it possible to spin the car around and around a full 540 degrees.

789
01:05:04,641 --> 01:05:09,640
I think the height of the show for the stunt drivers is doing the 540.

790
01:05:09,641 --> 01:05:12,640
When you get up into second and third gear and you're doing a high speed,

791
01:05:12,641 --> 01:05:17,640
and you can spin the car around and then put it into reverse and backwards,

792
01:05:17,641 --> 01:05:20,640
just back up the car the same speed, it's pretty intense.

793
01:05:20,641 --> 01:05:23,640
You feel the force in the seat.

794
01:05:23,641 --> 01:05:28,640
Even the tires were designed to meet the specific requirements of the show.

795
01:05:28,641 --> 01:05:33,640
They're overinflated to 80 pounds of pressure for better spinning and sliding,

796
01:05:33,641 --> 01:05:37,640
and so they can travel on two wheels without blowing out the sidewalls.

797
01:05:37,641 --> 01:05:42,640
It makes for good stunts, but the tires don't last long.

798
01:05:42,641 --> 01:05:46,640
The tires on these cars will burn off like every 15 minutes on a regular surface.

799
01:05:46,641 --> 01:05:51,640
Here on the stage, we'll have to change them every two shows, so they wear down really, really quick.

800
01:05:51,641 --> 01:05:57,640
The stuntman's costumes were just as carefully designed as the cars in the set.

801
01:05:57,641 --> 01:06:04,640
To make up for the lack of air conditioning and the fierce Florida heat, the drivers wear water-cooled vests.

802
01:06:04,641 --> 01:06:07,640
This is that cool suit that we wear underneath our fire suit.

803
01:06:07,641 --> 01:06:09,640
It's got little hoses that run all through it.

804
01:06:09,641 --> 01:06:13,640
They connect to these two hoses, which plug into our water system.

805
01:06:13,641 --> 01:06:16,640
And through these hoses, it circulates cold water from the cooler.

806
01:06:16,641 --> 01:06:20,640
And it's a pretty wild feeling when you first turn it on. It's pretty weird.

807
01:06:21,640 --> 01:06:28,460
But it's awesome, and it lasts for about 30 minutes and keeps your core temperature cold when you're driving in these cars, because it does get really, really hot.

808
01:06:31,740 --> 01:06:35,920
During each show, one stuntman was going to get particularly hot.

809
01:06:36,700 --> 01:06:41,900
The burning man, who catches on fire when his motorcycle skids through a wall of flames.

810
01:06:44,140 --> 01:06:48,040
The equipment that the guy wears can burn forever, pretty much. It's awesome.

811
01:06:48,041 --> 01:06:53,640
First come four layers of body armor and Nomex, a fire retardant thermal barrier.

812
01:06:55,400 --> 01:07:01,880
And then on top of that Nomex, he'll put on a racing suit like this, a three-layer racing suit that's soaked in cool gel.

813
01:07:02,300 --> 01:07:04,600
And the cool gel acts as a really major, like, thermal barrier.

814
01:07:05,140 --> 01:07:11,160
Then on top of this, he'll wear a burn suit, which is made of a special kind of denim that's stitched with Nomex.

815
01:07:11,980 --> 01:07:15,180
And then that's where he'll put the fuel on and eventually light him up.

816
01:07:15,181 --> 01:07:20,680
The show officially opened on May 5, 2005.

817
01:07:20,681 --> 01:07:26,500
From the stands, it looks like everybody's about to wreck, but they're actually putting

818
01:07:26,501 --> 01:07:28,800
their cars right in their exact mark.

819
01:07:28,801 --> 01:07:36,740
It's pretty spectacular to see.

820
01:07:36,741 --> 01:07:44,300
Coming up, Walt Disney World's newest park, which is home to a tree that's 14 stories

821
01:07:44,301 --> 01:07:51,020
and building that tree took some truly spectacular engineering magic.

822
01:07:54,760 --> 01:07:59,760
The Lights, Motors, Action, Extreme Stunt Show came to Walt Disney World

823
01:07:59,761 --> 01:08:02,620
as part of Disney's happiest celebration on Earth.

824
01:08:02,980 --> 01:08:05,020
50 years of theme park magic.

825
01:08:05,880 --> 01:08:09,000
Modern Marvel's Walt Disney World will return in a moment.

826
01:08:14,300 --> 01:08:26,340
Disney's Animal Kingdom, Walt Disney World's fourth and largest theme park, opened in April 1998.

827
01:08:27,720 --> 01:08:37,280
Covering more than 500 acres, this mammoth theme park is almost the size of Epcot, the Magic Kingdom and Disney Studios combined.

828
01:08:38,340 --> 01:08:41,880
This final park would have amazed even Walt himself.

829
01:08:41,881 --> 01:08:48,880
Here, audio-animatronic dinosaurs face cataclysmic extinction hundreds of times a day.

830
01:08:48,881 --> 01:08:55,880
And in the park's centerpiece attraction, Kilimanjaro Safaris,

831
01:08:55,881 --> 01:09:00,880
wild animals range across 110 acres of transplanted African savanna

832
01:09:00,881 --> 01:09:03,880
with what appears to be complete freedom of movement.

833
01:09:03,881 --> 01:09:10,880
Kilimanjaro Safaris is a huge dry land version of the Jungle Cruise at Disneyland

834
01:09:10,881 --> 01:09:17,600
at Disneyland, but the way Walt Disney envisioned it a half century earlier, using real animals.

835
01:09:20,860 --> 01:09:26,640
Walt was fascinated with wild animals. When he built Disneyland back in the 1950s,

836
01:09:26,740 --> 01:09:31,860
he originally planned to put actual hippos, giraffes, and monkeys along the banks of the

837
01:09:31,861 --> 01:09:32,679
Jungle Cruise.

838
01:09:32,680 --> 01:09:37,240
As we know, animals do whatever they want, so you couldn't, in the short space

839
01:09:37,241 --> 01:09:41,760
of time that people would be on the ride in these small areas that they would be looking at,

840
01:09:42,160 --> 01:09:47,380
make sure that these animals would be doing what you'd like them to do or in a place you'd like

841
01:09:47,381 --> 01:09:52,131
them to be. So AAs had to fill the bill at the time.

842
01:09:52,132 --> 01:09:56,060
But for Disney's Animal Kingdom, Imagineers

843
01:09:56,061 --> 01:10:01,692
finally had the resources and the technology to fully realize Walt's vision.

844
01:10:01,693 --> 01:10:03,100
Kilimanjaro Safaris

845
01:10:03,101 --> 01:10:06,800
gives the appearance of being a wild environment

846
01:10:06,801 --> 01:10:08,340
that you drive through in a vehicle

847
01:10:08,341 --> 01:10:10,220
and you see these animals roaming around.

848
01:10:10,940 --> 01:10:13,300
In fact, it is a show set.

849
01:10:13,440 --> 01:10:16,120
It's a series of scenes that have been constructed

850
01:10:16,121 --> 01:10:18,780
through the manipulation of the landscape environment.

851
01:10:19,600 --> 01:10:22,800
There is some edge somewhere that the animals cannot go beyond.

852
01:10:23,300 --> 01:10:26,080
So either those edges are invisible to you, the viewer,

853
01:10:26,360 --> 01:10:29,300
or in many cases, you're looking right at the boundary,

854
01:10:29,720 --> 01:10:31,900
but you don't recognize it as a boundary.

855
01:10:31,901 --> 01:10:39,400
It might look like a mud earth bank of a river or a little row of rocks or you might just

856
01:10:39,401 --> 01:10:44,360
notice a little shadow in the grass where unseen to you there could be a hidden moat

857
01:10:44,361 --> 01:10:47,020
or trench or fence.

858
01:10:47,021 --> 01:10:50,140
Working with wild animals provided some challenges.

859
01:10:50,141 --> 01:10:55,480
To encourage the animals to stay within sight of the vehicles, the Imagineers employed creative

860
01:10:55,481 --> 01:10:58,660
tricks, particularly for the lions.

861
01:10:58,661 --> 01:11:05,360
We do a whole variety of things, from providing artificial heat, artificial cooling, sunlight,

862
01:11:05,361 --> 01:11:10,240
shade, shadow water, food, things to do.

863
01:11:10,241 --> 01:11:11,240
Animals are like people.

864
01:11:11,241 --> 01:11:13,640
They get used to things, and you have to try new things.

865
01:11:13,641 --> 01:11:15,740
So we were misting for a while.

866
01:11:15,741 --> 01:11:18,880
There was this very cool mist that would just land on the rocks up where the lions would

867
01:11:18,881 --> 01:11:21,300
sit and they would like that and they'd sit there for a while.

868
01:11:21,301 --> 01:11:24,880
And then they got bored with that, and we switched to shade.

869
01:11:24,881 --> 01:11:29,140
By the time the Imagineers started building Disney's Animal Kingdom, they had a lot of

870
01:11:29,141 --> 01:11:33,940
experience at turning Florida swampland into pristine theme parks.

871
01:11:33,941 --> 01:11:39,360
But here, they wanted to create greater authenticity on a grander scale than they had ever before

872
01:11:39,361 --> 01:11:40,360
attempted.

873
01:11:40,361 --> 01:11:44,780
This was essentially a flat cow pasture.

874
01:11:44,781 --> 01:11:47,380
We actually had cows grazing on the site.

875
01:11:47,381 --> 01:11:53,400
We had to actually create every hill, mountain, elevation change that you see on the site.

876
01:11:53,401 --> 01:11:58,520
60 dump trucks of dirt were delivered to the construction site every day for two years

877
01:11:58,521 --> 01:12:00,160
straight.

878
01:12:00,161 --> 01:12:06,800
All told, Disney brought in 4.4 million cubic yards of dirt, enough to fill Spaceship Earth

879
01:12:06,801 --> 01:12:08,800
19 times.

880
01:12:08,801 --> 01:12:15,320
With the site prepared, Disney proceeded to install some 4 million plants.

881
01:12:15,321 --> 01:12:22,100
Virtually every living plant that you see in the park was planted by us.

882
01:12:22,101 --> 01:12:26,860
Some they grew themselves from seed, but in accelerators that produced full-sized plants

883
01:12:26,861 --> 01:12:29,620
in a fraction of the usual time.

884
01:12:29,621 --> 01:12:33,580
And some they moved from elsewhere in Walt Disney World.

885
01:12:33,581 --> 01:12:36,860
There were existing stocks of big trees.

886
01:12:36,861 --> 01:12:40,240
They had been planted like 25 years ago.

887
01:12:40,241 --> 01:12:42,960
And since they're on our property, we can move them.

888
01:12:42,961 --> 01:12:47,220
So we were picking up some big trees.

889
01:12:47,221 --> 01:12:53,940
The park's biggest and most significant tree measures 145 feet tall.

890
01:12:53,941 --> 01:12:59,220
That's 14 stories high with a trunk 50 feet in diameter.

891
01:12:59,221 --> 01:13:02,340
It's called the Tree of Life.

892
01:13:02,341 --> 01:13:09,280
But the Tree of Life isn't a real tree, and it's far from alive.

893
01:13:09,281 --> 01:13:15,320
Like Cinderella Castle and Spaceship Earth, the Tree of Life is a highly engineered, technologically

894
01:13:15,321 --> 01:13:20,040
sophisticated landmark that dominates the park.

895
01:13:20,041 --> 01:13:24,980
Early designs called for a tree about 50 feet high, something kids could climb and play

896
01:13:24,981 --> 01:13:25,980
on.

897
01:13:25,981 --> 01:13:33,200
Gradually, though, the tree took root and grew, on paper, to three times that height.

898
01:13:33,201 --> 01:13:36,640
But as the tree grew, so did the problems.

899
01:13:36,641 --> 01:13:42,660
For one thing, the Imagineers wanted to tuck an underground theater into the root system.

900
01:13:42,661 --> 01:13:47,700
We needed to create a structure that could not only support the branches and the leaves

901
01:13:47,701 --> 01:13:52,220
that you see on the tree, but also to create a 450-seat theater inside of it.

902
01:13:52,580 --> 01:13:57,700
So, of course, we had to have a free span structure inside, but yet a massive structure

903
01:13:57,701 --> 01:13:58,580
to support the tree.

904
01:13:59,080 --> 01:14:03,580
One of our engineers came and said, I have a solution to this problem, and it's really

905
01:14:03,581 --> 01:14:04,281
easy.

906
01:14:04,360 --> 01:14:06,040
The engineering has been done.

907
01:14:06,820 --> 01:14:07,600
I go, what's that?

908
01:14:07,601 --> 01:14:13,860
He goes, we'll just use the pre-engineered structure for a Gulf of Mexico oil platform,

909
01:14:14,200 --> 01:14:18,560
which is pre-engineered to all the stresses we would ever have and is the right shape.

910
01:14:19,300 --> 01:14:21,040
It was a brilliant solution.

911
01:14:21,840 --> 01:14:26,160
The rig was not only strong, it provided plenty of space at the base for the theater.

912
01:14:27,120 --> 01:14:30,820
In short order, an oil rig was on its way to central Florida.

913
01:14:31,240 --> 01:14:35,180
We actually barged it across the Gulf of Mexico, trucked it from Tampa,

914
01:14:35,181 --> 01:14:41,420
and erected it in one big piece down on the foundations, on the anchor bolts,

915
01:14:41,520 --> 01:14:42,960
and I'm happy to say it fit perfectly.

916
01:14:43,700 --> 01:14:46,360
To transform the massive rig into a tree,

917
01:14:46,860 --> 01:14:49,880
steel workers wrapped the structure in a second layer of steel

918
01:14:49,881 --> 01:14:53,000
to approximate the contours of a trunk and branches,

919
01:14:53,340 --> 01:14:55,420
which would eventually be encased in concrete.

920
01:14:57,280 --> 01:15:01,840
Besides the 12 main branches, the tree has 45 secondary branches,

921
01:15:01,841 --> 01:15:06,840
756 tertiary branches and some 8,000 end branches.

922
01:15:06,841 --> 01:15:10,840
When the wind blows, the limbs sway realistically,

923
01:15:10,841 --> 01:15:13,840
thanks to expansion joints at the base of the main branches.

924
01:15:13,841 --> 01:15:19,840
103,000 specially designed vinyl leaves rustle in the breeze.

925
01:15:19,841 --> 01:15:26,840
Our R&D team developed working with various manufacturers to withstand ultraviolet light and wind.

926
01:15:26,841 --> 01:15:32,360
and wind and as you can see six years later the tree still looks like it did

927
01:15:32,361 --> 01:15:38,280
the day we built it. The tree was also artistically challenging. An intricate

928
01:15:38,281 --> 01:15:43,100
montage of more than 300 animal sculptures is carved into the contours of

929
01:15:43,101 --> 01:15:48,060
the trunk. It's like a child's find the hidden picture game scaled up to

930
01:15:48,061 --> 01:15:56,580
skyscraper size. What we did was to sculpt the animals into a foam model of

931
01:15:56,581 --> 01:16:02,580
the tree and that sculpting was done really kind of inspirationally like what does that blob of

932
01:16:02,581 --> 01:16:08,900
tree remind me of and you know gee we don't have enough crabs on the tree let's do some crabs and

933
01:16:08,901 --> 01:16:15,240
that kind of thing very artistic very much an aesthetic exercise once the artists have done

934
01:16:15,241 --> 01:16:22,580
their work the technical team took over then we sectioned the tree off into little cubes and each

935
01:16:22,581 --> 01:16:28,820
Each of those little cubes was scanned, and those scans were used to create giant metal

936
01:16:28,821 --> 01:16:35,260
and lath cubes, which were then helicoptered in and placed around the tree, and then they're

937
01:16:35,261 --> 01:16:36,640
welded together.

938
01:16:36,641 --> 01:16:43,400
And what you end up with is a metal cage replica, full size, of the little model that you had

939
01:16:43,401 --> 01:16:45,800
sculpted.

940
01:16:45,801 --> 01:16:51,700
Artists spent the next 12 months, rain or shine, sculpting the network of animals.

941
01:16:51,701 --> 01:16:56,520
Applying a thin coat of cement to the wire framework, an artist would consult a batch

942
01:16:56,521 --> 01:17:01,840
of photos and begin to shape the soft concrete, racing to finish the animal's likeness in

943
01:17:01,841 --> 01:17:05,880
the six or seven hours before the concrete hardened.

944
01:17:05,881 --> 01:17:10,140
Once the sculptures were complete, a year to the day after they were begun, the tree

945
01:17:10,141 --> 01:17:15,100
was painted a mottled brown and gray and then topped with a protective clear coat to ward

946
01:17:15,101 --> 01:17:18,300
off ultraviolet fading.

947
01:17:18,301 --> 01:17:25,300
As huge and complex as the tree of life is, it's soon to be dwarfed, both physically and

948
01:17:25,301 --> 01:17:33,160
technologically, by Expedition Everest.

949
01:17:33,161 --> 01:17:37,940
Some of the trees on the African savanna are actually 30-foot tall southern live oaks,

950
01:17:37,941 --> 01:17:38,940
native to Florida.

951
01:17:38,941 --> 01:17:44,400
They've been given crew cuts to make them look like Africa's acacia trees.

952
01:17:44,401 --> 01:17:47,500
Modern Marvel's Walt Disney World will return in a moment.

953
01:18:03,520 --> 01:18:07,120
Dinosaurs have been roaming through Disney stories since 1940

954
01:18:07,440 --> 01:18:10,880
when the animated movie Fantasia featured them fighting to the death.

955
01:18:10,881 --> 01:18:19,360
For us, they really do fit into this balance of scientific and fantastic.

956
01:18:20,240 --> 01:18:26,040
We like to bring to life things that, prior to going to a Disney theme park,

957
01:18:26,340 --> 01:18:29,360
really only existed in your imagination or that you saw in a film.

958
01:18:29,780 --> 01:18:30,920
And a dinosaur fits that.

959
01:18:31,860 --> 01:18:36,820
So in designing Disney's Animal Kingdom, it was only fitting to include dinosaurs.

960
01:18:36,821 --> 01:18:41,820
But as usual, the Imagineers decided to plus it.

961
01:18:41,821 --> 01:18:47,820
They're some of the largest, smoothest, fastest creatures that anybody has ever made.

962
01:18:47,821 --> 01:18:53,820
They owe their speed and motion to yet another advance in audio animatronic technology.

963
01:18:53,821 --> 01:19:02,820
We went from a 500 PSI hydraulic system on the oldest figures, or 100 PSI pneumatic, to 3,000 PSI.

964
01:19:02,821 --> 01:19:05,820
So that meant we could put smaller cylinders in.

965
01:19:05,821 --> 01:19:09,820
Because they're at higher pressures, they carry a much higher load.

966
01:19:09,821 --> 01:19:12,820
Plus, we added compliance on all those figures.

967
01:19:12,821 --> 01:19:17,820
So we can get faster speeds, have smaller packages, and have them very fluid.

968
01:19:17,821 --> 01:19:22,820
In Dinosaur, people journey back in time to capture an Iguanodon,

969
01:19:22,821 --> 01:19:27,820
which, since this is Disney World, means a massive audio-animatronic dinosaur.

970
01:19:27,821 --> 01:19:38,840
Disney's Imagineers wanted to create a thrilling sense of action to make people feel like they were climbing boulders, dodging leadiers, and getting stuck in the mud.

971
01:19:39,900 --> 01:19:44,420
The solution was to build the thrills into the ride vehicle itself.

972
01:19:45,340 --> 01:19:48,200
We named it the EMV, the Enhanced Motion Vehicle.

973
01:19:48,500 --> 01:19:51,440
And this particular vehicle has a lot of capabilities.

974
01:19:51,660 --> 01:19:53,560
It has four-wheel steering.

975
01:19:53,980 --> 01:19:56,040
So right off the bat, you're not your typical truck.

976
01:19:56,041 --> 01:20:02,200
The four-wheel steering allows for tight turns and quick maneuvering, which simulates driving

977
01:20:02,201 --> 01:20:04,000
through very rough terrain.

978
01:20:04,001 --> 01:20:09,760
The real action, though, comes from hydraulic actuators that move the car body itself, much

979
01:20:09,761 --> 01:20:12,280
like in audio animatronics.

980
01:20:12,281 --> 01:20:19,540
What we have here is a motion-based vehicle that is actuated by 3,000 psi of hydraulic

981
01:20:19,541 --> 01:20:20,940
pressure.

982
01:20:20,941 --> 01:20:27,900
We have 100-gallon tanks that hold the hydraulic fluid, and from there it's sent out through

983
01:20:27,901 --> 01:20:33,300
hydraulic lines to the actuators themselves, controlled by the onboard computer.

984
01:20:33,301 --> 01:20:37,340
We have the ability to perfectly coordinate the motions of the vehicle with things that

985
01:20:37,341 --> 01:20:39,280
are happening around the vehicle.

986
01:20:39,281 --> 01:20:43,440
So when something falls next to you and causes a little explosion, you can have the actual

987
01:20:43,441 --> 01:20:47,160
vehicle lurch as if it's reacting to the things around you.

988
01:20:47,161 --> 01:20:55,520
The result is a thrilling race through time and space, dodging meteors and fierce dinosaurs.

989
01:20:55,521 --> 01:21:00,220
Hydraulic pressure provides the realistic motion in dinosaur for both the vehicles and

990
01:21:00,221 --> 01:21:01,220
the creatures.

991
01:21:01,221 --> 01:21:08,700
For decades, it's been the best way to animate the inanimate.

992
01:21:08,701 --> 01:21:13,620
But when Imagineers decided to create the world's most realistic audio-animatronic,

993
01:21:13,621 --> 01:21:16,860
they had to turn to new technology.

994
01:21:16,861 --> 01:21:23,860
The groundbreaking result is Lucky, the first free-roaming audio-animatronic ever.

995
01:21:23,861 --> 01:21:30,440
Lucky is a completely different type of technology than our in-ride audio-animatronic creatures

996
01:21:30,441 --> 01:21:37,400
who technologically their chief goal is to do the same thing over and over again forever

997
01:21:37,401 --> 01:21:42,540
and ever and ever and the engineering is aimed at this idea.

998
01:21:42,541 --> 01:21:48,400
Because Lucky's chief goal is to react smoothly and convincingly and direct his attention

999
01:21:48,401 --> 01:21:56,860
to individuals so that they know, that dinosaur's looking at me.

1000
01:21:56,861 --> 01:22:04,760
It's the biggest leap in AA since Lincoln first stood at the 1964 World's Fair.

1001
01:22:04,761 --> 01:22:10,200
To do this, we had to pretty much reinvent every way that we have created audio electronics

1002
01:22:10,201 --> 01:22:11,660
in the past.

1003
01:22:11,661 --> 01:22:16,240
we wanted to free Lucky up from the ground, from the floor, and have him walk around the

1004
01:22:16,241 --> 01:22:20,300
parks, we went with electric.

1005
01:22:20,301 --> 01:22:25,480
Disney employed carbon fiber technology, similar to that used in bicycles and airplanes, to

1006
01:22:25,481 --> 01:22:28,560
create a strong, lightweight frame.

1007
01:22:28,561 --> 01:22:33,220
And they formulated a new lightweight latex compound for the skin.

1008
01:22:33,221 --> 01:22:37,060
Because it's electric, we have to make sure that everything is lighter.

1009
01:22:37,061 --> 01:22:41,660
That skin is what we call a latex foam and it's a very lightweight, structured skin to

1010
01:22:41,661 --> 01:22:46,040
where if we tried to use any of our other skins that we've used in the past, we couldn't

1011
01:22:46,041 --> 01:22:51,060
because the weight was too high and the electrical cylinders and motors wouldn't handle that

1012
01:22:51,061 --> 01:22:52,060
load.

1013
01:22:52,061 --> 01:22:56,420
This is the first skin where the guests can walk right up to and actually touch.

1014
01:22:56,421 --> 01:23:00,340
So the feel of the figure actually had to feel like what you would think a dinosaur

1015
01:23:00,341 --> 01:23:02,380
would feel like.

1016
01:23:02,381 --> 01:23:06,740
Disney also came up with some innovative and highly proprietary software.

1017
01:23:08,860 --> 01:23:13,720
This is where we pushed the innovation beyond what anybody had done prior to us.

1018
01:23:14,620 --> 01:23:17,380
This had to be operated by one person.

1019
01:23:17,800 --> 01:23:21,860
Everything from an eye blink to walking to turning his head,

1020
01:23:22,500 --> 01:23:26,820
all of those had to be controlled simultaneously in real time in front of guests.

1021
01:23:27,140 --> 01:23:28,600
That actually is impossible.

1022
01:23:28,601 --> 01:23:37,280
So to pull it off, we wrote a very sophisticated piece of software that actually co-puppets the figure with the human.

1023
01:23:37,720 --> 01:23:44,480
So it's a human and a piece of software or a computer that is bringing Lucky to life whenever you see him out on stage.

1024
01:23:44,900 --> 01:23:50,320
The results are remarkably lifelike, even to the people who created him.

1025
01:23:51,340 --> 01:23:54,840
I kid you not, the team that works on Lucky take Lucky very seriously.

1026
01:23:55,260 --> 01:23:57,600
They view this as a living creature.

1027
01:23:57,601 --> 01:24:00,400
They ask how he's doing in the morning, how does he feel.

1028
01:24:01,500 --> 01:24:03,660
There's only one lucky so far,

1029
01:24:03,840 --> 01:24:07,500
and he's spending time in several Disney theme parks around the world.

1030
01:24:08,280 --> 01:24:12,600
In the fall of 2005, he helped open Hong Kong Disneyland.

1031
01:24:15,580 --> 01:24:19,440
One reason Walt loved his theme parks was because, as he put it,

1032
01:24:19,680 --> 01:24:20,900
they would never be finished.

1033
01:24:21,720 --> 01:24:24,960
They would always be evolving, improving, and growing.

1034
01:24:24,961 --> 01:24:31,920
One of the most ambitious new attractions now taking shape is Expedition Everest, whose

1035
01:24:31,921 --> 01:24:38,760
steel-frame summit towers 199 feet above Disney's Animal Kingdom, more than 50 feet higher than

1036
01:24:38,761 --> 01:24:45,180
The Tree of Life, and 10 feet higher than Cinderella Castle. Expedition Everest will

1037
01:24:45,181 --> 01:24:50,640
contain a roller coaster thrill ride inside the mountain, and thus join Disney's long

1038
01:24:50,641 --> 01:24:54,194
chain of roller coasters housed inside mountains.

1039
01:24:54,195 --> 01:24:57,240
Space Mountain is the most famous, but the

1040
01:24:57,241 --> 01:25:00,178
first was the Matterhorn at Disneyland.

1041
01:25:00,179 --> 01:25:04,020
In fact, the Matterhorn made roller coaster history

1042
01:25:04,021 --> 01:25:06,140
when it debuted in 1959.

1043
01:25:06,141 --> 01:25:12,200
Roller coasters originally were always made of wood and they would have flat steel plates

1044
01:25:12,201 --> 01:25:15,816
attached to the top of the wood and to the sides of the wood.

1045
01:25:15,817 --> 01:25:17,180
This had to go inside a

1046
01:25:17,181 --> 01:25:18,320
a Matterhorn mountain.

1047
01:25:18,321 --> 01:25:22,200
There was no way to do the conventional woodwork.

1048
01:25:22,201 --> 01:25:23,860
What's the easiest thing to bend?

1049
01:25:23,861 --> 01:25:27,100
Well, you know you can bend electrical conduit pretty simple.

1050
01:25:27,101 --> 01:25:29,180
They figured you could take pipe,

1051
01:25:29,181 --> 01:25:30,400
you could bend pipe,

1052
01:25:30,401 --> 01:25:31,720
and then you could weld it together

1053
01:25:31,721 --> 01:25:34,620
with ties between the rails.

1054
01:25:34,621 --> 01:25:36,600
It was nothing more than a simple expedient

1055
01:25:36,601 --> 01:25:38,980
of how to get this done easy.

1056
01:25:38,981 --> 01:25:41,440
Voila, they accidentally invented the first

1057
01:25:41,441 --> 01:25:43,740
of that type of roller coaster.

1058
01:25:47,180 --> 01:25:54,660
Since then, almost all roller coasters have been made of curved steel, allowing for tighter turns and faster speeds.

1059
01:25:56,800 --> 01:26:01,180
Expedition Everest will be made the same way, but on a huge scale.

1060
01:26:02,040 --> 01:26:05,720
The roller coaster is a traditional gravity roller coaster.

1061
01:26:05,900 --> 01:26:11,560
It uses a chain lift and very, very large motors to lift the train to the top of the mountain,

1062
01:26:11,561 --> 01:26:20,180
where gravity then takes over and we have all the stored energy of a train full of passengers at 100 feet in the air

1063
01:26:20,181 --> 01:26:24,700
and the amount of energy that that large mass has at that height.

1064
01:26:25,280 --> 01:26:30,040
Creating a mountain to enclose a ride this huge is no easy task.

1065
01:26:30,940 --> 01:26:33,060
The mountain is a complicated structure.

1066
01:26:33,640 --> 01:26:36,200
On the outside, it looks like a mountain. That's very nice.

1067
01:26:36,300 --> 01:26:38,960
But in fact, it's three independent architectural systems.

1068
01:26:38,961 --> 01:26:43,760
There's a rigid system, which holds up the concrete of the mountain and is by definition

1069
01:26:43,761 --> 01:26:46,080
absolutely rigid.

1070
01:26:46,081 --> 01:26:52,280
There's a flexible dynamic system, which is the ride track, which because this multi-ton

1071
01:26:52,281 --> 01:26:55,440
vehicle is moving on it, that ride track has to move.

1072
01:26:55,441 --> 01:26:57,920
But it can never touch the mountain.

1073
01:26:57,921 --> 01:27:01,620
Those two systems can't touch each other, but they have to be built simultaneously so

1074
01:27:01,621 --> 01:27:05,800
the steel of one is actually penetrating through the steel of the other.

1075
01:27:05,801 --> 01:27:10,280
And if you put one little piece of steel in at the wrong time, you've stopped your entire

1076
01:27:10,281 --> 01:27:11,740
construction process.

1077
01:27:11,741 --> 01:27:17,300
We poured a very, very large matte foundation over an acre of concrete, over three feet

1078
01:27:17,301 --> 01:27:21,220
thick to spread the load of both of those two structures.

1079
01:27:21,221 --> 01:27:26,360
And then we began the construction of the roller coaster and the exoskeleton, the mountain

1080
01:27:26,361 --> 01:27:29,980
static structure at the same time.

1081
01:27:29,981 --> 01:27:35,560
Further complicating things is a giant yeti, the mythical guardian of the mountains.

1082
01:27:35,561 --> 01:27:42,700
The Yeti is so big that the Yeti is sitting on his own independent architectural structure

1083
01:27:42,701 --> 01:27:45,300
that goes all the way to the footings of the building

1084
01:27:45,301 --> 01:27:50,680
and cannot touch either the ride structure or the existing architectural structure of the mountain.

1085
01:27:50,800 --> 01:27:55,220
And all of these things had to be simultaneously constructed because they're all big

1086
01:27:55,221 --> 01:27:58,320
and you can't put one in after you've built the other one.

1087
01:27:59,400 --> 01:28:02,020
Disneyland's Matterhorn also features a Yeti.

1088
01:28:02,021 --> 01:28:07,460
But the one at Expedition Everest will be both huge and incredibly nasty.

1089
01:28:08,420 --> 01:28:11,540
For now, he's a closely guarded secret.

1090
01:28:12,540 --> 01:28:20,500
Our Yeti is gritty, real Yeti with, you know, slobber and wet legs from walking in out of the snow

1091
01:28:20,501 --> 01:28:24,960
and matted dreadlock hair and, you know, a real animal.

1092
01:28:25,060 --> 01:28:30,100
An animal that you see for a very brief moment, but when you see it, you think, wow, was that really real?

1093
01:28:30,101 --> 01:28:36,180
Once Everest's basic structures were in place, workers began creating the rockwork.

1094
01:28:37,180 --> 01:28:42,360
Although this mountain is no more encased in rock, then the tree of life is covered in bark.

1095
01:28:42,960 --> 01:28:48,500
The mountain structure is covered with over five acres of themed carved rockwork.

1096
01:28:49,220 --> 01:28:54,020
The matrix of plaster that's carved to replicate as closely as possible

1097
01:28:54,021 --> 01:28:57,520
the geological rock forms that you'd find in the Himalayas.

1098
01:28:57,521 --> 01:29:02,080
From here we're actually seeing some of the scaffolding on the exterior of the mountain.

1099
01:29:02,081 --> 01:29:06,880
That scaffolding is used to provide access to the workers to install the lath,

1100
01:29:06,881 --> 01:29:09,760
which is the matrix that the plaster is applied to,

1101
01:29:09,761 --> 01:29:13,360
and then carve that plaster and then finally paint it.

1102
01:29:13,361 --> 01:29:18,480
Thousands and thousands of construction workers from Central Florida working on the project,

1103
01:29:18,481 --> 01:29:22,480
and it's like conducting an orchestra.

1104
01:29:22,481 --> 01:29:29,800
After three years of construction, the mountain will finally be complete, and Expedition Everest

1105
01:29:29,801 --> 01:29:31,940
will open to the public.

1106
01:29:31,941 --> 01:29:39,700
At Disney, it's been said, no good idea is ever allowed to die.

1107
01:29:39,701 --> 01:29:43,140
Expedition Everest seems to bear that out.

1108
01:29:43,141 --> 01:29:49,500
It's another huge expansion on the theme park concept Walt first unveiled at Disneyland.

1109
01:29:49,501 --> 01:29:57,080
A lot has happened in the four decades since Walt Disney first sketched out his idea for his biggest and most ambitious project ever.

1110
01:29:58,700 --> 01:30:05,580
Walt Disney World is huge and complex in both its overall vision and its day-to-day operations.

1111
01:30:06,760 --> 01:30:13,520
It's not exactly what Walt described to the world when the veil of secrecy was lifted from his mysterious Florida project.

1112
01:30:13,521 --> 01:30:22,160
But it's the closest thing on earth to a real-life embodiment of Walt's biggest, grandest, most magical dream.

